{
  "case_id": "2007-06-8273",
  "document_hash": "c76faf93c4d835c3eb143a6106bc3d71187abf7ef04eaff61d79bd200fb077b8",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2007-06-8273",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "c76faf93c4d835c3eb143a6106bc3d71187abf7ef04eaff61d79bd200fb077b8",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c76faf93c4d8-0000-0000",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 1,
        "start": 1
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The above-entitled matter came on for oral argument before the Supreme Court of the United States at 10:01 a.m."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2007-06-8273",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "c76faf93c4d835c3eb143a6106bc3d71187abf7ef04eaff61d79bd200fb077b8",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c76faf93c4d8-0000-0001",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 1,
        "start": 1
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "APPEARANCES: BENJAMIN J. BUTLER, ESQ., Assistant Minnesota State Public Defender, Minneapolis, Minn.; on behalf of the Petitioner. PATRICK C. DIAMOND, ESQ., Deputy County Attorney, Minneapolis, Minn.; on behalf of the Respondent."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Benjamin J. Butler",
      "case_id": "2007-06-8273",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "c76faf93c4d835c3eb143a6106bc3d71187abf7ef04eaff61d79bd200fb077b8",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c76faf93c4d8-0000-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. BUTLER: Mr. Chief Justice, and may it please the Court: In this case, the Minnesota Supreme Court held that this Court had prevented it from deciding for itself which State prisoners can go into Minnesota State courts to raise Federal constitutional challenges to their conviction. This is incorrect."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Benjamin J. Butler",
      "case_id": "2007-06-8273",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "c76faf93c4d835c3eb143a6106bc3d71187abf7ef04eaff61d79bd200fb077b8",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c76faf93c4d8-0000-0005",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 6,
        "start": 6
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. BUTLER: Your Honor, what the Teague Court did was set up a procedural and a prudential limit on a defense available for the State in the particular form of habeas corpus."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Benjamin J. Butler",
      "case_id": "2007-06-8273",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "c76faf93c4d835c3eb143a6106bc3d71187abf7ef04eaff61d79bd200fb077b8",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c76faf93c4d8-0000-0006",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 13,
        "start": 13
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. BUTLER: No, Your Honor. First of all, what Hudson -- the Hudson case asked was whether exclusion of evidence was required under the Fourth Amendment when the violation of the Fourth Amendment was a no-knock violation."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Souter",
      "case_id": "2007-06-8273",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "c76faf93c4d835c3eb143a6106bc3d71187abf7ef04eaff61d79bd200fb077b8",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c76faf93c4d8-0000-0007",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 19,
        "start": 19
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOUTER: No. But you are also relying, as I understand it, on State common law, in effect. And you are saying, that so long as the State common law does not give less by way of remedy and relief than the Federal decision requires, the State is free, as a matter of State remedial common law, to do more. That's your point, isn't it?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2007-06-8273",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "c76faf93c4d835c3eb143a6106bc3d71187abf7ef04eaff61d79bd200fb077b8",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c76faf93c4d8-0001-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 24,
        "start": 24
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SCALIA: Then do you think the State is free to decide how and when and whether it will, quote, apply?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "State",
      "case_id": "2007-06-8273",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "c76faf93c4d835c3eb143a6106bc3d71187abf7ef04eaff61d79bd200fb077b8",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c76faf93c4d8-0001-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 26,
        "start": 26
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Justice, is that if there is a violation of the test of the Sixth Amendment confrontation clause, this Court decides what remedies are required, what remedies for certain people and perhaps for other people what remedies are not required."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "State of Minnesota",
      "case_id": "2007-06-8273",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "c76faf93c4d835c3eb143a6106bc3d71187abf7ef04eaff61d79bd200fb077b8",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c76faf93c4d8-0001-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 30,
        "start": 30
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Federal law controls the retroactivity or non-retroactivity of new constitutional rulings. This Court determines the constitutional requirements --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Souter",
      "case_id": "2007-06-8273",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "c76faf93c4d835c3eb143a6106bc3d71187abf7ef04eaff61d79bd200fb077b8",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c76faf93c4d8-0001-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 33,
        "start": 33
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOUTER: And the finality point in effect establishes a way of calculating the application of a rule in any given State in any given case, but it will not result in the uniform application of the rule. Isn't that correct?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kennedy",
      "case_id": "2007-06-8273",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "c76faf93c4d835c3eb143a6106bc3d71187abf7ef04eaff61d79bd200fb077b8",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c76faf93c4d8-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 38,
        "start": 38
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Well, I must say I have serious problems with your position in that regard."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Diamond",
      "case_id": "2007-06-8273",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "c76faf93c4d835c3eb143a6106bc3d71187abf7ef04eaff61d79bd200fb077b8",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c76faf93c4d8-0002-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 38,
        "start": 38
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Your Honor, I think that it's difficult. If you look at, for example, the recent case in Minnesota, Krasky, dealing with essentially the same evidence that's at issue in this case, if the State was adopting Krasky as some State standard, then this Court wouldn't be in a position to review it."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2007-06-8273",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "c76faf93c4d835c3eb143a6106bc3d71187abf7ef04eaff61d79bd200fb077b8",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c76faf93c4d8-0002-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 38,
        "start": 38
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The question I have is, to go back, you said two separate things. One is you say the question here is a matter of Federal law. I'll give you that. It's a matter of Federal law."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Stevens",
      "case_id": "2007-06-8273",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "c76faf93c4d835c3eb143a6106bc3d71187abf7ef04eaff61d79bd200fb077b8",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c76faf93c4d8-0002-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 48,
        "start": 47
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "May I ask another sort of basic question? The Teague rule is a Federal rule -- the Teague ruling. What is your understanding of the source of that rule? Is it the Court's power to announce, make Federal common law? Or is it a constitutionally mandated rule?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Source extraction",
      "case_id": "2007-06-8273",
      "confidence": 0.0,
